Z||000000||ezsurf||06-08-99||06:52 PM||||<br>I'm just about ready to put my 2.8 engine back in my car.  The main power harness connector (rectangular in shape approx. 2 in. by 4 in.) is caked full of what I would call an electrical sealant.  This stuff is black and extermely gooey.  I believe I'm going to have to clean this stuff out to ensure good electrical contacts.  Does anyone know if you have to replace this stuff with fresh goo?  I don't know if this stuff is to prevent arcing, assist in heat removal, or prevent moisture.  Would I hurt anything if I just cleaned this stuff out and didn't replace this goo? 
Z||000001||theogre||06-08-99||07:33 PM||||that's a "dielectric" compound. it prevents all the above depending on just what connector it's in. well.... not arching per se, it prevents that by keeping corosion from opening up the connector.<p>It should be replaced. There is a clear silicon based grease often used in ignition wires that does the same thing. You can get the clear stuff a radio shack or any other electronic supplier or auto parts store.
